WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:03.170
Multi-factor authentication or MFA

1
00:00:03.329 --> 00:00:05.469
is one of the strongest protections for your account,

2
00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:07.250
but attackers know this too,

3
00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:09.409
which is why they now try to trick people

4
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:12.949
into approving MFA requests they did not start.

5
00:00:13.329 --> 00:00:16.309
This is called MFA manipulation.

6
00:00:16.569 --> 00:00:19.489
One common method is MFA fatigue,

7
00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:22.090
also known as MFA bombing.

8
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.530
This happens when an attacker repeatedly sends

9
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.219
login approval prompts to your phone,

10
00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:29.829
hoping you will tap approve just to make the notification stop.

11
00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:31.479
Remember this simple rule,

12
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.279
if you did not try to sign in,

13
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.029
do not approve the request.

14
00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:38.229
Attackers use fake login pages that look official.

15
00:00:38.369 --> 00:00:39.450
If you enter your credentials,

16
00:00:39.529 --> 00:00:42.590
they can bypass security and access your account directly.

17
00:00:42.889 --> 00:00:43.840
To protect yourself,

18
00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.450
always check the website address before signing in.

19
00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:48.909
Be cautious of links sent through email,

20
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:49.889
Teams,

21
00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:50.610
Slack,

22
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.319
text messages or unexpected pop-ups.

23
00:00:53.689 --> 00:00:54.479
When in doubt,

24
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.220
go directly to the official website or app instead of using the link.

25
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:02.700
They may also try to add a new MFA device to your account.

26
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.750
Always report any unfamiliar security setting

27
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.180
changes or new device alerts immediately.

28
00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:12.830
Be alert if you're asked to switch to a weaker sign-in method like text messages.

29
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.059
Attackers target these methods because they are easier to intercept.

30
00:01:17.199 --> 00:01:18.949
If this happens unexpectedly,

31
00:01:19.269 --> 00:01:20.589
stop and report it.

32
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.949
Attackers often prefer weaker methods because they are easier to manipulate.

33
00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.589
Here are 4 simple habits that help keep your account safe.

34
00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.629
Never approve an MFA request

35
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:32.970
you did not start.

36
00:01:34.699 --> 00:01:36.580
Do not share one-time codes with anyone,

37
00:01:36.699 --> 00:01:39.160
even if they claim to be from IT or support.

38
00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:42.489
Use approved password managers and passkeys where available as

39
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:44.650
they help protect you from fake login pages.

40
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.580
Report repeated MFA prompts,

41
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.330
unexpected login alerts,

42
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.019
or unfamiliar security changes straight away.

43
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.779
MFA is powerful,

44
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.370
but it still needs your attention.

45
00:01:56.879 --> 00:01:58.410
Pause before you approve,

46
00:01:58.839 --> 00:02:00.059
check before you click,

47
00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:01.699
and when something feels unusual,

48
00:02:02.199 --> 00:02:02.720
report it.